    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S7386A\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the retirement and social security\nlaw, in relation to the transfer of members between public retirement\nsystems; and to repeal subdivisions g and i of section 43 of such law\nrelating thereto\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nThis bill amends subdivisions b and d of section 43 of the retirement\nand social security law to remove the one year time limitation for\nmembers to give notice of their intention to transfer between\nretirement systems, as well as the time limit for such transfers to be\naffected.\n\nSubdivision b of section 43 is further amended to remove outdated\nreferences to transfers occurring before nineteen hundred fifty-nine.\n\nSubdivision d of section 43 is further amended to eliminate the\nrequirement that a transferee acquire at least three years of service\nin the second retirement system in order to earn the better benefit.\n\nThis bill also repeals subdivisions g and i of section 43 of the\nretirement and social security law in their entirety.\n\n REASONS FOR SUPPORT :\n\nThe proposed legislation simplifies the administration of transfers\nbetween the pension systems within New York State.\n\nFirst, the proposal to remove the one-year time limit for members to\ngive notice of their intent to transfer between retirement systems, as\nset forth in subdivision b of section 43, would allow members adequate\ntime to evaluate the desirability of a transfer without imposing any\nadditional administrative burden on the retirement systems. As many\nstate and city government positions impose a probationary period of\none year or more for new employees, a one-year time limit places an\nunnecessary burden on such members. The proposed amendment would also\nserve to alleviate administrative burdens by reducing multiple\ntransfers by members who change employment several times. The proposal\nto repeal the portions of subdivision b relating to transfers\noccurring before nineteen hundred fifty-nine would serve to simplify\nthe statute by eliminating outdated language that does not affect any\ncurrent members.\n\nSecond, this bill removes the framework, set forth in subdivision d of\nsection 43, which requires that a member accrue at least three years\nof service in the second retirement system in order to earn the better\nbenefit between the two retirement systems, and which prevents a\nmember with less than three years of service in the second retirement\nsystem to become entitled to a greater or lesser benefit than the\nmember would have been entitled to in the first retirement system.\nDerived from the use of Final Salary in computing pension benefits for\nTier 1 members, this statute was originally designed to address the\naugmentation of a member's pension benefit where the member earned a\nhigher salary in the second retirement system but accrued fewer than\nthree years of service with the higher salary, thereby achieving a\nfinal salary that was significantly higher than the salary earned\nthroughout the majority of the member's service.  This provision is no\nlonger necessary due to the Final Average Salary (FAS) calculations\nused for Tier 4 and Tier 6 members, which provide for an averaging-out\nof final salary during an extended period of a member's employment.\nThe Kingston Limitation, which provides that wages earned in any one\nyear used in the FAS computation cannot exceed the average of the\nprevious two years by more than ten percent, further serves to\nmitigate an augmentation in pension benefits caused by a spike in\nsalary. Tier 6 members have additional caps on the salary that are\nused in calculating the FAS which further supersedes the limitations\nset forth in this statute. These include a cap on earnings to no more\nthan the governor's salary and a cap on overtime earnings to be used\nin the FAS. The proposed amendment will simplify the statute and its\nimplementation without altering the intent of the law.\n\nThird, the proposal to eliminate subdivisions g and i of section 43 in\ntheir entirety would further simplify the statute by removing outdated\nlanguage that no longer affects members of any of the retirement\nsystems.\n\nThis bill was introduced at the request of the NYCERS Board of\nTrustees.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE OF THE BILL :\n\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS: OF THE BILL :\n\nAccording to the Chief Actuary for NYCERS, NYCTRS and NYCBERS in\nFiscal Note 2016-16, enactment of this proposed legislation is\nexpected to result in an increase in employer contributions of no more\nthan $25,000 per year, in total, to NYCERS, NYCTRS and NYCBERS.\n\nAccording to the Actuary of the NYS Teachers' Retirement system the\ncost is negligible.\n\nAccording to the Actuary of The New York State and Local Retirement\nSystem the annual cost is negligible.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n",
